Gynaecomastia
Young Adult & Adolecence
Palpable subareolar rubbery mass, Concentric enlargement around nipple, Nipple tenderness, Absence of skin dimpling

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-021
Reason for Visit: Acute panic and severe localized chest pain secondary to the rapid enlargement of a unilateral subareolar mass.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a highly distressed adolescent reporting a new, acutely painful, and rapidly enlarging lump in the chest. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly necessary to rule out an acute soft tissue abscess or neoplastic process, and to provide immediate clinical de-escalation and diagnostic clarification prior to routine clinical hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "There is a hard, burning lump right under my nipple that hurts so much when my shirt rubs against it, and I am terrified it is cancer."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a one-week history of a progressively tender, firm mass beneath the right nipple. The onset correlates with recent mid-puberty hormonal shifts. Initially manifesting as mild sensitivity, the localized pain has escalated sharply over the past 48 hours, causing severe psychosocial distress and anxiety regarding the chest appearance. The patient reports extreme tenderness of the nipple-areolar complex when subjected to friction from clothing. The patient notes a palpable rubbery mass extending concentrically outward from the nipple, but denies any pathological nipple discharge or history of trauma to the area.
Functional Status: Ambulating, but holding the right arm rigidly across the chest to prevent friction.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute health anxiety; active avoidance of physical activity and school due to fear of the mass being noticed.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/72 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Patient is visibly anxious, guarded, and reluctant to expose the chest for examination.
Physical Exam: Chest examination reveals a palpable subareolar rubbery mass measuring approximately 2cm on the right side. The mass demonstrates concentric enlargement around the nipple. There is exquisite nipple tenderness on light palpation. Crucially, there is a strict absence of skin dimpling, pathological discharge, or hard, fixed, irregular borders. Axillary lymphadenopathy is absent. Genitourinary examination (chaperoned) confirms normal external male genitalia. There is a clear discrepancy between the localized palpable glandular tissue and generalized adiposity.
Environmental Observations: Patient is wearing multiple layers of loose clothing to hide the chest.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications. No recent use of illicit substances or anabolic steroids.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Unilateral Pubertal Gynaecomastia - Status: Stable, highly symptomatic
Differentials:

Breast Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of overlying cellulitic erythema, fluctuance, fever, or purulent discharge.

Male Breast Carcinoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to the mass being rubbery, concentric, and mobile, rather than a hard, fixed, eccentric lesion with associated skin dimpling or tethering.

Lipoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to the exquisite tenderness and the firm, glandular texture directly tethered to the areolar complex, rather than a soft, painless, mobile subcutaneous fat collection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (High psychological risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe health anxiety and localized chest pain driven by the rapid, unilateral development of subareolar glandular tissue. The fear of malignancy or severe infection caused acute psychological decompensation. Delaying this assessment until standard daytime hours would have left a highly vulnerable adolescent in a state of unmanaged panic and physical discomfort. The immediate after-hours home visit allowed for a comprehensive, reassuring physical examination that safely excluded acute suppurative or neoplastic pathologies, confirming a benign physiological pubertal process and providing immediate emotional de-escalation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided direct, factual clinical reassurance regarding the benign and common nature of pubertal gynaecomastia. Recommended over-the-counter NSAIDs for acute localized pain. Ordered non-urgent baseline fasting serology (testosterone, oestradiol, prolactin, beta-hCG, and LFTs) via the regular GP to formally rule out rare pathological causes.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically protect the hypersensitive nipple from painful friction, wear a tight-fitting, compressive athletic undershirt, directly neutralizing the sheer forces from loose clothing that trigger the pain response. To reduce psychological hypervigilance, explicitly limit self-examination to once a week in the shower, acknowledging that constant prodding actively increases local inflammation and pain.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to discuss blood test results and provide ongoing psychological support and monitoring.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department is required if the lump rapidly turns red, hot, swollen, and begins leaking pus, or if the patient develops a high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinically consistent with normal pubertal development; baseline blood tests are required to ensure hormone levels are balanced.
Clinical Course: The acute tenderness will gradually subside over the next few months, and the glandular tissue itself frequently resolves completely within 1 to 2 years as pubertal hormones stabilize.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if the skin over the lump turns bright red and hot to the touch, if you develop a fever, or if you notice a bloody discharge from the nipple.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The lump is a normal response of your chest tissue to the sudden surge of hormones during puberty. It is not an infection and it is definitively not cancer. The pain is simply the tissue stretching and reacting to friction.
Actionable Steps: Wear a compression shirt under your daily clothes to stop the pain caused by your shirt rubbing the area. Stop pressing on the lump daily, as this physical trauma keeps the area inflamed and painful.

SMS INFORMATION

